 p-cltdg-can-criminal-conversations-be-genuine\nDate/time: \n\n Thursday 19
  November 2026 \n\n 6:15pm to 7:15pm\n\n Location: \n\n Faculty of Law\, G
 28 (The Beckwith Moot Court Room)\n\n About \n\n Speaker: Joaquin Casalia 
 (LSE)\n Abstract: Most legal academics analysing populism focus exclusivel
 y on formal legal changes. Some even suggest that constitutional scholars 
 should keep in mind that it is more important to see what populists are do
 ing than what they are saying. However\, we act with words. As language ph
 ilosopher J.L. Austin demonstrated\, to say something is usually to do a n
 umber of things.\n Currently\, democratic guardrails of constitutions are 
 undermined not only by amendments to its provisions but also by political 
 actors making them ‘dead letter.’ Speech act theory in general\, and f
 eminist philosophy of language in particular\, provide valuable tools to e
 xplain (i) how populism can use speech to silence institutions and the opp
 osition\, 
END:VEVENT
