 , B16\n\n About \n\n Speaker: Dr Matt Hasler (Teaching By-Fellow in Law an
 d Philosophy\, Churchill College)\n Family law systems characteristically 
 impose relational obligations: legal duties of financial provision that at
 tach to personal or familial relationships\, and thereby impose special fi
 nancial responsibilities on their bearers. Relational obligations pose a h
 ost of important normative questions. To which kinds of relationships shou
 ld they attach? What should their content be? And to what extent\, if at a
 ll\, should parties be entitled to alter or disapply such obligations by m
 eans of private contracts?\n It is often suggested that our answers to the
 se questions will be significantly determined by our attitudes to two appa
 rently competing principles. On the one hand\, respect for individual auto
