 .law.cam.ac.uk/press/events/2026/12/cambridge-legal-theory-discussion-grou
 p-cltdg-does-consent-matter-only-indirectly-sex-crimes\nDate/time: \n\n Tu
 esday 1 December 2026 \n\n 6:45pm to 7:45pm\n\n Location: \n\n Faculty of 
 Law\, G24\n\n About \n\n Speaker: Adam Kolber (Brooklyn Law School)\n Abst
 ract: Most legal academics analysing populism focus exclusively on formal 
 legal changes. Some even suggest that constitutional scholars should keep 
 in mind that it is more important to see what populists are doing than wha
 t they are saying. However\, we act with words. As language philosopher J.
 L. Austin demonstrated\, to say something is usually to do a number of thi
 ngs.\n Currently\, democratic guardrails of constitutions are undermined n
 ot only by amendments to its provisions but also by political actors makin
 g them ‘dead letter.’ Speech act theory in general\, and feminist phil
 osophy of language in particular\, provide valuable tools to explain (i) h
 ow populism can use speech to silence institutions and the opposition\, an
 d (ii) how populist
END:VEVENT
